<section xml:id="notes-week-11" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Week 11</title>

<introduction>
    <p>
        This is an outline of the topics we covered in Week 11 of the course, from November 9 to November 13.
    </p>
</introduction>

<handout component="cur">
    <title>Monday 11/9</title>
    <objectives>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Activity on Polynomial Division and Domains
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Solving Polynomial Inequalities
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Solving Rational Inequalities
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>
    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Polynomial Division and Domains of Rational Functions</title>

<p>
    Last week, we talked about how we could use polynomial division to find zeros of functions, and how to analyze rational functions to find their domains. Today, we'll start with an activity about that.
</p>

        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Polynomial and Rational Inequalities</title>
            
            <p>
                Sometimes, a model or situation will want us to solve a polynomial or rational inequality, instead of just an equation.
            </p>

            <example workspace="0.1in">
                <introduction>
                    <p>
                        Analyze each of the following inequalities using their graphs.
                    </p>
                </introduction>
                <task>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            <m>x^2 \gt 4</m>
                        </p>
                        <image width="60%">
                <shortdescription>Graph of a polynomial.</shortdescription>
                <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
             label="poly-ineq-one">
            <diagram xmlns="https://prefigure.org" dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
  <definition> f(x)=x^2</definition>
  <coordinates bbox="[-4,-4,4,10]">
    <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
    <graph function="f"/>
  </coordinates>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
                    </statement>
            
                </task>
                <task>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            <m>\frac{x}{x^2 - 3} \leq 2</m>
                        </p>
                        <image width="60%">
                <shortdescription>Graph of a polynomial.</shortdescription>
                <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
             label="poly-ineq-two">
            <diagram xmlns="https://prefigure.org" dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
  <definition> f(x)=x/(x^2 - 3)</definition>
  <coordinates bbox="[-5,-10,5,10]">
    <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
    <graph function="f"/>
  </coordinates>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
                    </statement>
            
                </task>
            
            </example>
            
        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <p component="b" workspace="4in">
            The summary is that we need to be very careful in how we approach these things. What does the general process look like?
        </p>
        <p component="a">
            The summary is that we need to be very careful in how we approach these things. What does the general process look like? Add and subtract to get zero on one side. Make a sign chart for the function that is left. 
        </p>

    </page>

    <page>
        <example workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Solve the inequality <md>
                        x^2 \gt 4
                    </md>.
                    
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>

    <page>
        <example workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Solve the inequality <md>
                        x^2 + x + 1 \leq 4x + 11
                    </md>.
                    
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>

    <page>
        <example workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Solve the inequality <md>
                        x-2 \leq 4 - x^2
                    </md>.
                    
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>

    <page>
        <p>
            This also works for higher degree polynomials; we just need to be able to figure out where that polynomial is zero.
        </p>

        <example workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Solve the inequality
                    <md>
                        x^3 + 2x^2 - 1 \lt x^2 + 4x + 3
                    </md>.
                    
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>
    <page>
        <example workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Solve the inequality
                    <md>
                        x^4 - 15x^2 + x \geq 5x^2 + x - 7 - x^4
                    </md>.
                    
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
    </page>

</handout>

<handout component="cur">
    <title>Wednesday 11/11</title>
    <objectives>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Review Polynomial Inequalities
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Solve Rational Inequalities
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>

 <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Polynomial and Rational Inequalities</title>
            
            <p>
                Sometimes, a model or situation will want us to solve a polynomial or rational inequality, instead of just an equation.
            </p>

            <example workspace="0.1in">
                <introduction>
                    <p>
                        Analyze each of the following inequalities using their graphs.
                    </p>
                </introduction>
                <task>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            <m>x^2 \gt 4</m>
                        </p>
                        <image width="60%">
                <shortdescription>Graph of a polynomial.</shortdescription>
                <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
             label="poly-ineq-one-two">
            <diagram xmlns="https://prefigure.org" dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
  <definition> f(x)=x^2</definition>
  <coordinates bbox="[-4,-4,4,10]">
    <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
    <graph function="f"/>
  </coordinates>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
                    </statement>
            
                </task>
                <task>
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            <m>\frac{x}{x^2 - 3} \leq 2</m>
                        </p>
                        <image width="60%">
                <shortdescription>Graph of a polynomial.</shortdescription>
                <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
             label="poly-ineq-two-two">
            <diagram xmlns="https://prefigure.org" dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
  <definition> f(x)=x/(x^2 - 3)</definition>
  <coordinates bbox="[-5,-10,5,10]">
    <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
    <graph function="f"/>
  </coordinates>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
                    </statement>
            
                </task>
            
            </example>
            
        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <p component="b" workspace="4in">
            The summary is that we need to be very careful in how we approach these things. What does the general process look like?
        </p>
        <p component="a">
            The summary is that we need to be very careful in how we approach these things. What does the general process look like? Add and subtract to get zero on one side. Make a sign chart for the function that is left. 
        </p>

    </page>

        <page>
        <example workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Solve the inequality <md>
                        x^2 \gt 4
                    </md>.
                    
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>

    <page>
        <example workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Solve the inequality <md>
                        x^2 - x + 1 \leq 4x + 7
                    </md>.
                    
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>

    <page>
        <example workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Solve the inequality <md>
                        x-4 \lt 8 - x^2
                    </md>.
                    
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>

 <!--    
    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Polynomial Inequalities</title>
