        Last time, we dealt with solving polynomial inequalities, problems of the form <md>
            x^2 - 3x - 5 \geq 5-x 
        </md>.
        What was the general process for solving these?
    </p>
    <p component="a">
        Last time, we dealt with solving polynomial inequalities, problems of the form <md>
            x^2 - 3x - 5 \geq 5-x 
        </md>.
        What was the general process for solving these? Get zero on one side, sign chart, read the answer.
    </p>

        </paragraphs>
    </page>
 -->
        <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Rational Inequalities</title>
            <p component="b" workspace="4in">
                What happens if rational functions or fractions are involved? Overall, the process is still the same.
            </p>
            <p component="a">
                What happens if rational functions or fractions are involved? Overall, the process is still the same. The main warning is don't clear denominators until you're solving the equation version. Places where the function is not defined matter a lot.
            </p>
        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <example workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Solve the inequality
                    <md>
                        \frac{4x}{x-3} \gt 0
                    </md>.
                    
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
    </page>

    <page>
        <example workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Solve the inequality
                    <md>
                        \frac{x}{x^2-3} \leq 2
                    </md>.
                    
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
    </page>

    <page>
        <example workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Solve the inequality
                    <md>
                        \frac{x-1}{x+6} \lt \frac{x-4}{x+2}
                    </md>.
                    
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
    </page>

    <page>
        <example workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Solve the inequality
                    <md>
                        \frac{x+3}{2x-5} \geq 2
                    </md>. Note: The answers here will involve fractions.
                    
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
    </page>

    <page>
        <example workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Solve the inequality
                    <md>
                        \frac{x - \frac{7}{3}}{x+1} \lt 1 + \frac{x - \frac{16}{3}}{x-2}
                    </md>.
                    
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
    </page>

    <page>
        <example workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Solve the inequality
                    <md>
                        \frac{2}{x^2 - 4x + 3} \leq \frac{5}{x^2 - 9}
                    </md>.
                    
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
    </page>

    <page>
        <p>
            Next, we'll review all of this with an activity.
        </p>
    </page>
</handout>


<handout component="cur">
    <title>Friday 11/13</title>
    <objectives>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Recap on polynomial and rational inequalities
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Quiz on 4.3 and 4.5 (polynomial division and rational functions)
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>
    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Polynomial and Rational Inequalities</title>

        <p component="b" workspace="4in">
            What did we cover this week? What are the key ideas behind solving these inequalities?
        </p>
        <p component="a">
            What did we cover this week? What are the key ideas behind solving these inequalities? Not as simple as linear inequalities where we can just solve for <m>x</m>. We need to get zero on one side of the inequality and then build a sign chart for the resulting function. We can then use that to read off the answer.
        </p>

        </paragraphs>
    </page>
    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Where are we heading next?</title>

            <p component="b" workspace="4in">
                After this week, we're leaving polynomials behind for a bit. Instead, we're going to look at a different class of functions and see what kind of physical problems we can solve with them.
            </p>
            <p component="a">
                After this week, we're leaving polynomials behind for a bit. Instead, we're going to look at a different class of functions and see what kind of physical problems we can solve with them. Our new class of functions are the exponential functions <m>a^x</m> and their inverses, logarithmic functions. <m>x^2</m> vs. <m>2^x</m>.
            </p>
            
        </paragraphs>
    </page>
</handout>

</section>